WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:04.992
Every second of every day, billions of invisible decisions are being made inside the

00:05.039 --> 00:06.269
devices around you.

00:06.513 --> 00:10.762
And behind almost all of them is a piece of silicon smaller than your fingernail,

00:10.971 --> 00:15.093
shaped by one question: what is this chip actually for?

00:15.593 --> 00:17.694
If you've shopped for a computer, a phone,

00:17.718 --> 00:21.619
or a graphics card lately, you've run into a wall of acronyms.

00:21.793 --> 00:27.807
CPU, GPU, TPU, NPU, FPGA, ASIC.

00:27.981 --> 00:29.095
They all compute.

00:29.339 --> 00:30.895
They all sit on silicon.

00:31.336 --> 00:35.330
So why are there so many of them, and why does it suddenly matter which one is

00:35.388 --> 00:36.677
inside your machine?

00:37.177 --> 00:39.406
Here's the core idea in one image.

00:39.580 --> 00:43.110
For fifty years, computing was a Swiss Army knife problem.

00:43.435 --> 00:46.895
We built one brain that could do everything reasonably well.

00:47.220 --> 00:48.752
That era is ending.

00:49.077 --> 00:52.096
Modern systems are becoming a collection of specialists,
